! **THE WIDTH IS MEASURED AND NOT MATCHED, and the first attempt at this fixture
! got it wrong in a way only the moment showed.**  Setting swidth to the Gaussian
! fixture's 0.05 Ha matches a NUMBER and not a temperature: under Fermi-Dirac
! that is kT = 1.36 eV, which destroys the magnetism outright.  Both codes then
! converge, agree with each other to six decimals, and are comparing nothing --
! Elk's total moment came back at 1.7e-04 and defumat's int|m| at 0.0020, against
! 0.54 and 0.515 on the Gaussian pair.  That is this project's "a check whose null
! result cannot be told from a pass" exactly: two codes agreeing about a
! nonmagnetic chain.
!
! So the width is chosen from a sweep of the QE side rather than copied
! (defumat, q = 0, conv_thr 1e-10):
!
!     degauss (Ry)   int|m|     -TS (Ry)
!          0.10      0.00195   -0.141049     <- magnetism gone
!          0.05      0.32206   -0.050193     <- half suppressed
!          0.02      0.90181   -0.002055     <- this fixture
!          0.01      0.92881   -0.000048
!          0.005     0.92955   -0.000000055
!
! 0.02 Ry is where the moment has reached its low-temperature value, 0.90 of the
! saturated 0.93, while the entropy is still 28 meV and therefore still large
! enough that the convention this pair exists to neutralise would matter if it
! were not neutralised.  Below 0.01 the term vanishes and the fixture would prove
! nothing; above 0.05 the magnet does.
!
! Note the low-temperature moment is 0.93 and the Gaussian pair's is 0.515, so
! **that pair's smearing was itself suppressing the moment by nearly half**.  The
! two pairs are different physical states and no number crosses between them.
!
! The Fermi-Dirac twin of h-chain-spiral-elk.in, and the cell to prefer for any
! new spiral comparison against Elk.
!
! **It differs in one word, and that word is why the pair exists.** Elk computes
! its entropy term only for Fermi-Dirac smearing (energy.f90:242,
! IF (stype == 3)), so with the Gaussian its total is an INTERNAL energy where
! this code's and QE's are FREE energies, and E(q) - E(0) means a different thing
! on the two sides. On the Gaussian pair, at its degauss = 0.1 Ry, that gap is
! 112 meV between q = 0 and
! q = 1/4, five times the difference being compared, and it is the whole of the
! factor of five PLAN.md P86 could not place and P105 resolved. With fermi-dirac
! on this side and stype = 3 on Elk's, both totals carry -TS and the two can be
! subtracted directly.
!
! degauss is 0.02 Ry here and NOT the Gaussian pair's 0.1: see the sweep at the
! head of this file for why, which is that 0.1 Ry of Fermi-Dirac destroys the
! magnetism outright. For this scheme degauss is kT and Elk's swidth is kT in
! Hartree, so 0.02 Ry is swidth = 0.01 Ha, which is what the Elk twin carries.
!
! **The state is not the Gaussian cell's and no number should be carried across.**
! A different smearing function is a different free-energy surface, so the moment,
! the energy and E(q) - E(0) all move; what this fixture buys is that the two
! codes are on the same surface as each other, which the other pair was not.
!
! The QE-side twin of tests/data/elk/h_chain_spiral/elk.in: the SAME hydrogen
! chain spiral, set up so that the two codes differ by their basis and nothing
! else that can be helped.
!
! One line differs from h-chain-spiral.in and it is input_dft. Elk's Perdew-
! Zunger LDA (xctype = 2) is implemented spin-unpolarised only and Elk refuses a
! magnetic run with it, so the functional both codes can do is Perdew-Wang
! (Elk's default xctype = 3, QE's 'pw'). The dataset is still H.pz-vbc.UPF, a
! PZ-generated pseudopotential run under PW -- an inconsistency that exists on
! the QE side alone, Elk being all-electron, and one hydrogen tolerates because
! it has no core.
!
! Also changed: conv_thr and the spiral wavevector are left where the scan sets
! them. **The inherited sentence about degauss staying at 0.1 Ry does not apply
! to this cell** and is replaced by the sweep above: this one runs 0.02. The
! caution it carried is still real -- at a small smearing this soft magnetic
! surface lands in whichever nearby minimum the starting density is closest to --
! and is why the sweep stops at 0.02 rather than going lower.
!
! PLAN.md P86 has the comparison.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 6, celldm(1) = 12.0, celldm(3) = 0.41666666666667,
    nat = 1, ntyp = 1,
    ecutwfc = 25.0,
    input_dft = 'pw'
    occupations = 'smearing', smearing = 'fermi-dirac', degauss = 0.02
    noncolin = .true.
    nosym = .true.
    starting_magnetization(1) = 0.6
    angle1(1) = 90.0
    spiral_q(1) = 0.0, spiral_q(2) = 0.0, spiral_q(3) = 0.25
    nbnd = 6
 /
 &electrons
    mixing_beta = 0.3
    conv_thr = 1.0d-11
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 H  1.008  H.pz-vbc.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS (crystal)
 H 0.0 0.0 0.0
K_POINTS {automatic}
 1 1 4 0 0 0
